The guest handle's make side: four calls a guest binds the same way in every language.
amk_var_get answers a name with its expanded value, allocated, or null when no such
variable exists, which expansion alone cannot say; amk_has_db says whether make has built
its tables yet, false under an engine flag; amk_var_set defines a simple variable with
the literal value; amk_eval reads text as makefile syntax. gmk_expand from make's own
load api, compiled whether or not load is enabled, is the read for everything else.

A write from a forked one-shot guest would die with the child, so the child writes each
one as a record to a temp file the parent opened before the fork, and the parent applies
the records, in order, once the child's output is in. In this process, a write applies at
once. The queue is a file and not a pipe so a guest that writes more than a pipe holds
cannot block against a parent still reading its output.

